        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: 死",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "死 — sǐ — \"to die\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "我的花都死了 wǒ de huā dōu sǐ le — All my flowers have died."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The new character, in pieces: 死 (sǐ) — six strokes: a long bar on top; under it on the left the evening piece of 外, three strokes; on the right a short sweep and a vertical that bends right along the foot and hooks up, as on the right of 比. The next lesson puts it in your hand."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "死 (sǐ) is inherited from Middle Chinese sijX (Guangyun, in Baxter's transcription), which quotes an older dictionary for it: 'to be used up', what a person parts from."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
